,"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"F-INVARIANT AND E-INVARIANT","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":691.4832307618286,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":208.8,"r":403.24988190108047,"t":700.43392272},"charspan":[0,27],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"F-INVARIANT AND E-INVARIANT"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"PEIGEN CAO","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":661.6073725,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":278.5199,"r":333.46297214498196,"t":669.5774825000001},"charspan":[0,10],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"PEIGEN CAO"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Abstract. F -invariant for a pair of good elements (e.g. cluster monomials) in cluster algebras is introduced by the author in a previous work. A key feature of F -invariant is that it is a coordinatefree invariant, that is, it is mutation invariant under the initial seed mutations. E -invariant for a pair of decorated representations of quivers with potentials is introduced by Derksen, Weyman and Zelevinsky, which is also a coordinate-free invariant. The strategies used to show the mutationinvariance of F -invariant and E -invariant are totally different.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":584.0874524999999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":120.9597,"r":491.1394073303785,"t":641.8575825},"charspan":[0,562],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"Abstract. F -invariant for a pair of good elements (e.g. cluster monomials) in cluster algebras is introduced by the author in a previous work. A key feature of F -invariant is that it is a coordinatefree invariant, that is, it is mutation invariant under the initial seed mutations. E -invariant for a pair of decorated representations of quivers with potentials is introduced by Derksen, Weyman and Zelevinsky, which is also a coordinate-free invariant. The strategies used to show the mutationinvariance of F -invariant and E -invariant are totally different."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In this paper, we give a new proof of the mutation-invariance of F -invariant following the strategy used by Derksen, Weyman and Zelevinsky. As a result, we prove that F -invariant coincides with E -invariant on cluster monomials. We also give a proof of Reading's conjecture, which says that the non-compatible cluster variables in cluster algebras can be separated by the sign-coherence of g -vectors.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":534.2874324999999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":120.96075999999994,"r":491.10276941029554,"t":582.0974025},"charspan":[0,403],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"In this paper, we give a new proof of the mutation-invariance of F -invariant following the strategy used by Derksen, Weyman and Zelevinsky. As a result, we prove that F -invariant coincides with E -invariant on cluster monomials. We also give a proof of Reading's conjecture, which says that the non-compatible cluster variables in cluster algebras can be separated by the sign-coherence of g -vectors."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Contents","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":483.35717048,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":281.88069999999993,"r":330.1136279958774,"t":492.41321023999996},"charspan":[0,8],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"Contents"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"1. Introduction","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":323.18919999999986,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":265.80048,"r":346.30732470693755,"t":333.1518399999998},"charspan":[0,15],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"1. Introduction"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Cluster algebras. Cluster algebras were introduced by Fomin and Zelevinsky [FZ02] as a combinatorial approach to the upper global bases of quantum groups and to the theory of total positivity in algebraic groups. A cluster algebra of rank n (with trivial coefficients) is a Z -subalgebra of a rational function field generated by a special set of generators called cluster variables , which are grouped into overlapping subsets of fixed size n , called clusters . A seed t = ( x t , B t ) is a pair consisting of a cluster x t = ( x 1; t , . . . , x n ; t ) and a skew-symmetrizable integer matrix B t = ( b t ij ) n × n , called an exchange matrix . One can obtain new seeds from a given one by a procedure called mutation . The sets of cluster variables and clusters of a cluster algebra are determined by an initial seed and the iterative mutations. A cluster momomial is a monomial in variables from the same cluster. The remarkable Laurent phenomenon [FZ02] says that the expansion of a cluster monomial u with repect to any cluster x t is a Laurent polynomial.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":185.66919999999982,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":85.07958000000002,"r":527.2181386512029,"t":315.1518399999998},"charspan":[0,1066],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"Cluster algebras. Cluster algebras were introduced by Fomin and Zelevinsky [FZ02] as a combinatorial approach to the upper global bases of quantum groups and to the theory of total positivity in algebraic groups. A cluster algebra of rank n (with trivial coefficients) is a Z -subalgebra of a rational function field generated by a special set of generators called cluster variables , which are grouped into overlapping subsets of fixed size n , called clusters . A seed t = ( x t , B t ) is a pair consisting of a cluster x t = ( x 1; t , . . . , x n ; t ) and a skew-symmetrizable integer matrix B t = ( b t ij ) n × n , called an exchange matrix . One can obtain new seeds from a given one by a procedure called mutation . The sets of cluster variables and clusters of a cluster algebra are determined by an initial seed and the iterative mutations. A cluster momomial is a monomial in variables from the same cluster. The remarkable Laurent phenomenon [FZ02] says that the expansion of a cluster monomial u with repect to any cluster x t is a Laurent polynomial."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The notions of g -vectors and F -polynomials associated to cluster monomials are introduced in [FZ07]. To each cluster monomial u and any initial seed t = ( x t , B t ), one has the g -vector g t u ∈ Z n and F -polynomial F t u ( y 1 , . . . , y n ) ∈ Z [ y 1 , . . . , y n ] with respect to seed t = ( x t , B t ). It is known from [GHKK18] that the F -polynomial F t u has non-negative coefficients and constant term 1. Fomin and Zelevinsky [FZ07] proved that the Laurent expansion of u with respect to seed t can be written as:","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":125.90899999999976,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":85.07965400000006,"r":527.0581516005502,"t":183.63183999999978},"charspan":[0,530],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"The notions of g -vectors and F -polynomials associated to cluster monomials are introduced in [FZ07]. To each cluster monomial u and any initial seed t = ( x t , B t ), one has the g -vector g t u ∈ Z n and F -polynomial F t u ( y 1 , . . . , y n ) ∈ Z [ y 1 , . . . , y n ] with respect to seed t = ( x t , B t ). It is known from [GHKK18] that the F -polynomial F t u has non-negative coefficients and constant term 1. Fomin and Zelevinsky [FZ07] proved that the Laurent expansion of u with respect to seed t can be written as:"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"u = x g t u t F t u (ˆ y 1; t , . . . , ˆ y n ; t ) , (1.1)","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":104.65709459999982,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":85.07866000000001,"r":350.63831128000004,"t":120.40192155999978},"charspan":[0,59],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"where ˆ y k ; t = x B t e k t and e k is the k th column of I n . We call (1.1) the canonical expression of u with respect to seed t .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":76.10898999999984,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":85.07826000000011,"r":526.9271521703475,"t":99.94250654999985},"charspan":[0,134],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/10","text":"where ˆ y k ; t = x B t e k t and e k is the k th column of I n . We call (1.1) the canonical expression of u with respect to seed t ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"footnote","orig":"2020 Mathematics Subject Classification. 13F60, 16G10.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":54.6476725,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":96.9602,"r":306.6711739880142,"t":62.617782499999976},"charspan":[0,54],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/11","text":"2020 Mathematics Subject Classification. 13F60, 16G10."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"1","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":43.10264831666666,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":303.9606,"r":307.92872065,"t":49.73245505},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/12","text":"1"}],"version":"1.10.0"}